# Project: Soccer Practice Search

## Project Overview
- Web app to search soccer training videos (YouTube) by keyword, purpose, player count, level, and channel
- Japanese-first UX; mobile-friendly layout matters
- Data lives in PostgreSQL (production); local dev uses `utilities/.env.local` when present
- Deployed on Fly.io (`soccer-practice-search`)

---

## Product Principles
- Help coaches and players find relevant drills quickly
- Prefer clarity and fast scanning over feature bloat
- Client-side features (favorites, practice notes, tactical board) may use `localStorage` — do not assume server-side persistence for those
- Respect CSRF on state-changing HTTP APIs from the browser

---

## Tech Stack
- Language: Python 3.x
- Backend: Flask, Flask-WTF (CSRF)
- Database: PostgreSQL (via `psycopg2`, connection pooling in `utilities/db_access.py`)
- Frontend: Jinja2 templates, vanilla JavaScript, shared `static/styles.css` (custom UI; Outfit font)
- External API: YouTube Data API (video metadata)
- Deployment: Fly.io (`fly.toml`, app name `soccer-practice-search`)

---

## UI / UX Rules
- Keep visual consistency with existing `static/styles.css` and `templates/partials/site_header.html`
- Prefer extending existing components and CSS variables over introducing a new CSS framework
- Test narrow viewports: long Japanese strings (e.g. upload dates) must not overflow cards (`min-width: 0`, wrapping)
- Sticky search bar and touch-friendly controls on mobile are intentional — preserve behavior when changing layout

---

## Architecture

### Responsibilities
- **HTTP / routes**: `app.py` (single Flask app, no blueprints in the current layout)
- **DB access**: `utilities/db_access.py` (pool, queries, channel name resolution)
- **Ingestion / batch**: `main.py` orchestrates DB setup and YouTube fetch pipelines; `utilities/get_videos.py`, `utilities/update_category_db.py`, etc.
- **Templates**: `templates/`; shared header in `partials/`
- **Static**: `static/` — `scripts.js` (search/cards), `practice-notes.js`, `favorites.js`, `tactical-board.js`, etc.

### Do not
- Commit secrets, `.env`, or API keys
- Add heavy front-end frameworks without an explicit product decision
- Bypass CSRF for JSON POST routes without a documented security review

---

## Coding Conventions

### Naming
- Python: `snake_case` modules and functions, `PascalCase` classes
- JS/CSS: follow existing file patterns (`card`, `search-primary`, …)

### Code Quality
- Small, reviewable diffs; match surrounding style
- Comments explain **why**, especially for YouTube quotas, DB assumptions, or Fly.io quirks

---

## Error Handling & External APIs
- YouTube API and network calls can fail, rate-limit, or return partial data — handle gracefully
- Log actionable messages; avoid logging secrets or full connection strings

---

## Security
- `SECRET_KEY` and `DATABASE_URL` from environment — never hardcode
- Use `git status` / `git diff` before commit; enable `scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit` when possible
- See `docs/CURSOR_RULES_SECURITY.md` and `SECURITY.md` for team expectations

---

## Testing
- Run `./scripts/release.sh` (or `.venv/bin/python -m pytest tests/ -q`) before shipping; full ship: `./scripts/release.sh --ship`
- Mock HTTP in tests (see `tests/test_get_videos.py`)

---

## Documentation
- Prefer updating `README.md` or `docs/` when setup or deploy steps change
- Do not add unsolicited markdown files unless the user asks

---

## AI Assistance
- Read nearby code before editing; preserve CSRF, env loading, and DB connection patterns
- Respond in the user’s conversation language when applicable (see `language-follow-input.mdc`)
